arXiv:2509.14952v1 [cs.LG] 18 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Stochastic Bilevel Optimization with Heavy-Tailed Noise</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhuanghua Liu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luo Luo
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper considers the smooth bilevel optimization in which the lower-level problem is strongly convex and the upper-level problem is possibly nonconvex.
We focus on the stochastic setting that the algorithm can access the unbiased stochastic gradient evaluation with heavy-tailed noise, which is prevalent in many machine learning applications such as training large language models and reinforcement learning.
We propose a nested-loop normalized stochastic bilevel approximation (N<sup id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>SBA) for finding an <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point with the stochastic first-order oracle (SFO) complexity of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\kappa^{\frac{7p-3}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}\epsilon^{-\frac{4p-2}{p-1}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\kappa^{\frac{7p-3}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}\epsilon^{-\frac{4p-2}{p-1}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is the condition number, <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in(1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in(1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> is the order of central moment for the noise, and <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise level.
Furthermore, we specialize our idea to solve the nonconvex-strongly-concave minimax optimization problem, achieving an <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point with the SFO complexity of <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\kappa^{\frac{2p-1}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}\epsilon^{-\frac{3p-2}{p-1}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\kappa^{\frac{2p-1}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}\epsilon^{-\frac{3p-2}{p-1}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>.
All above upper bounds match the best-known results under the special case of the bounded variance setting, i.e., <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the following smooth bilevel optimization problem</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\min_{x\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{x}}}&amp;\varphi(x)\coloneqq f(x,y^{*}(x)),\\
{\rm s.t.}~&amp;y^{*}(x)\coloneqq\arg\min_{y\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{y}}}g(x,y),\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\min_{x\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{x}}}&amp;\varphi(x)\coloneqq f(x,y^{*}(x)),\\
{\rm s.t.}~&amp;y^{*}(x)\coloneqq\arg\min_{y\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{y}}}g(x,y),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where both the upper-level function <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x,y)={\mathbb{E}}[F(x,y^{*}(x),\xi)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x,y)={\mathbb{E}}[F(x,y^{*}(x),\xi)]</annotation></semantics></math> and lower-level function <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x,y)={\mathbb{E}}[G(x,y,\zeta)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,y)={\mathbb{E}}[G(x,y,\zeta)]</annotation></semantics></math> are jointly continuous differentiable.
In this paper, we focus on the nonconvex-strongly-convex setting where the lower-level function <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and strongly convex with respect to <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, while the hyper-objective function <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth but possibly nonconvex.
This formulation is ubiquitous in a wide range of machine learning applications, including reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, hyperparameter optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, meta learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and neural architecture search <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There has been a growing research interest in developing stochastic algorithms for solving the large-scale bilevel problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Earlier studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> have utilized Hessian–vector product (HVP)-based methods, combined with random samples of gradients and Hessians, to directly approximate the hyper-gradient of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be explicitly characterized as</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\varphi(x)=\nabla_{x}f(x,y^{*}(x))-\nabla^{2}_{xy}g(x,y^{*}(x))(\nabla^{2}_{yy}g(x,y^{*}(x)))^{-1}\nabla_{y}f(x,y^{*}(x))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\varphi(x)=\nabla_{x}f(x,y^{*}(x))-\nabla^{2}_{xy}g(x,y^{*}(x))(\nabla^{2}_{yy}g(x,y^{*}(x)))^{-1}\nabla_{y}f(x,y^{*}(x)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Leveraging established convergence results of stochastic gradient descent for smooth nonconvex objectives, these methods are able to obtain an <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\epsilon^{-4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\epsilon^{-4})</annotation></semantics></math> HVP calls.
However, these approaches require access to the Jacobian and the inverse of the Hessian of the lower-level function, which can be computationally prohibitive in large-scale bilevel optimization problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the aforementioned HVP-based approaches, more recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> have proposed fully stochastic first-order methods for solving bilevel optimization problems, without requiring any second-order information.
Specifically, these methods leverage the following penalty reformulation of the bilevel problem (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Stochastic Bilevel Optimization with Heavy-Tailed Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y)\coloneqq f(x,y)+\lambda(g(x,y)-g^{*}(x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y)\coloneqq f(x,y)+\lambda(g(x,y)-g^{*}(x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the penalty parameter.
Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. [35]</a></cite> introduce the auxiliary function</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)\coloneqq{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x)),~{\rm where}~y_{\lambda}^{*}(x)=\arg\min_{y\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{y}}}{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>where</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)\coloneqq{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x)),~{\rm where}~y_{\lambda}^{*}(x)=\arg\min_{y\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{y}}}{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. [4]</a></cite> demonstrate that, by setting <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\Theta(\epsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\Theta(\epsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, an <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notably, the gradient of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed using only first-order information from <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, given by</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)=\nabla_{x}f(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x))+\lambda(\nabla_{x}g(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x))-\nabla_{x}g(x,y^{*}(x)))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)=\nabla_{x}f(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x))+\lambda(\nabla_{x}g(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x))-\nabla_{x}g(x,y^{*}(x))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">This reformulation effectively reduces the problem of finding an approximate stationary point of <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> to that of <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby enabling the development of fully stochastic first-order methods.
We note that the convergence analysis of these methods typically relies on the assumption that the stochastic gradients of both the upper-level and lower-level problems have bounded variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, recent insights in machine learning indicate that the bounded variance assumption is restrictive.
The empirical evidence from scenarios like image classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, large language model (LLM) training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, and policy optimization in reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> suggests that stochastic gradients often follow heavy-tailed distributions.
These findings suggest adopting a weaker noise model which only requires the <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-th bounded central moment (<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-BCM) of the stochastic gradient such that <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla F(x,y;\xi)-\nabla f(x,y)\right\|^{p}]\leq\sigma_{f}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla F(x,y;\xi)-\nabla f(x,y)\right\|^{p}]\leq\sigma_{f}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla G(x,y;\zeta)-\nabla g(x,y)\right\|^{p}]\leq\sigma_{g}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{E}}[\left\|\nabla G(x,y;\zeta)-\nabla g(x,y)\right\|^{p}]\leq\sigma_{g}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{g}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{g}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in(1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in(1,2]</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math>, this assumption reduces to the standard bounded gradient variance assumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
For <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, however, the variance of the stochastic gradient estimator may be unbounded, leading to heavy-tailed noise distributions such as the Pareto distribution or Lévy <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-stable distribution.
Although existing works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> establish non-asymptotic convergence guarantees for stochastic methods in bilevel optimization under the bounded variance assumption, the convergence behavior of such methods in the presence of more general heavy-tailed noise remains unclear.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>We compare the complexity bounds of our methods with those of existing baselines for solving bilevel optimization problems. The second column indicates whether the algorithm relies on the stochastic Hessian-vector product (SHVP) oracle or the stochastic first-order (SFO) oracle.
We denote <math id="S1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\max\{\sigma_{f},\sigma_{g}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\max\{\sigma_{f},\sigma_{g}\}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
