</span></span><span id="S1.T1.2.7.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">SFO</td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S1.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathcal{O}}}\left(\dfrac{\kappa^{\frac{7p-3}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}}{\epsilon^{\frac{4p-2}{p-1}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathcal{O}}}\left(\dfrac{\kappa^{\frac{7p-3}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}}{\epsilon^{\frac{4p-2}{p-1}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">(1,2]</td></tr>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">a,b</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm poly}(\kappa,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm poly}(\kappa,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> because <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al. [28]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. [35]</a></cite> do not provide the explicit dependence on <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">c</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. [4]</a></cite> established that F<sup id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>SBA attains an SFO complexity of <math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathcal{O}}}\left({\kappa^{12}\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{-6}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>12</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathcal{O}}}\left({\kappa^{12}\sigma^{2}}{\epsilon^{-6}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. This bound can be improved by a factor of <math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> through the application of the standard SGD analysis for strongly convex functions in Theorem D.2 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose an efficient stochastic first-order method called nested-loop normalized stochastic bilevel approximation (N<sup id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>SBA) for solving the nonconvex-strongly-convex bilevel optimization problem under the <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-BCM gradient noise condition.
Our algorithm employs a nested-loop structure so that the outer loop can be regarded as an inexact stochastic gradient descent on the nonconvex function <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)\coloneqq{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)\coloneqq{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y_{\lambda}^{*}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, while the inner loop approximately solves the subproblems <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda g(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda g(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> for a given <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To guarantee the convergence of our algorithm under the <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-BCM noise condition, the outer loop employs stochastic gradient normalization with respect to <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the inner loop applies clipped stochastic gradient descent to <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and an auxiliary variable <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
We rigorously establish that the N<sup id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>SBA method attains an <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\lambda}^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> within a stochastic first-order oracle (SFO) complexity of <math id="S1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\kappa^{\frac{7p-3}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}\epsilon^{-\frac{4p-2}{p-1}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\big(\kappa^{\frac{7p-3}{p-1}}\sigma^{\frac{p}{p-1}}\epsilon^{-\frac{4p-2}{p-1}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in expectation, where <math id="S1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the condition number and <math id="S1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\max\{\sigma_{f},\sigma_{g}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\max\{\sigma_{f},\sigma_{g}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the special case of bounded variance (i.e., <math id="S1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2</annotation></semantics></math>), the resulting SFO complexity <math id="S1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\kappa^{11}\sigma^{2}\epsilon^{-6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\kappa^{11}\sigma^{2}\epsilon^{-6})</annotation></semantics></math> of our method matches that of the state-of-the-art stochastic first-order method reported in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
Furthermore, we establish a high probability convergence guarantee of the N<sup id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>SBA algorithm under the heavy-tailed noise, which matches the complexity of its in-expectation counterpart up to a multiplicative logarithmic factor.
We compare the proposed methods with prior work for solving the nonconvex-strongly-convex bilevel optimization problem in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Stochastic Bilevel Optimization with Heavy-Tailed Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
